{:edb/db-before-t 1660 :edb/db-after-t 1661 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534973 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:52:55.580Z" 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096463 1071 "Luisa has an overdraft facility authorized by her bank. Her bank balance on Monday was $\\[math] 173.25$. On Tuesday, Luisa decided to withdraw $\\[/math] 250.70$ to buy a laptop. What is Luisa's bank balance at the end of the day on Tuesday?" 13194139534973 17592186045417 false] [17592186096463 1071 "Luisa has an overdraft facility authorized by her bank. Her bank balance on Monday was $\\[math] 173.25.$ On Tuesday, Luisa decided to withdraw $\\[/math] 250.70$ to buy a laptop. What is Luisa's bank balance at the end of the day on Tuesday?" 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096463 1074 "To find Luisa's bank balance at the end of the day on Tuesday, we subtract the amount of money Luisa spent on the laptop from the starting balance, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\[math] 173.25 - \\[/math] 250.70 = -\\[math] 77.45\n$$\n\nTherefore, Luisa's bank balance at the end of the day on Tuesday was $-\\[/math] 77.45$." 13194139534973 17592186045417 false] [17592186096463 1074 "To find Luisa's bank balance at the end of the day on Tuesday, we subtract the amount of money Luisa spent on the laptop from the starting balance, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\[math] 173.25 - \\[/math] 250.70 = -\\[math] 77.45\n$$\n\nTherefore, Luisa's bank balance at the end of the day on Tuesday was $-\\[/math] 77.45.$" 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096464 1071 "Nazareth is doing an experiment in her Chemistry class. She heated a liquid whose initial temperature was $-5.6^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$. After several minutes, the temperature of the liquid rose by $4.7^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$, at which point she decided to end the experiment. What was the final temperature of the liquid?" 13194139534973 17592186045417 false] [17592186096464 1071 "Nazareth is doing an experiment in her Chemistry class. She heated a liquid whose initial temperature was $-5.6^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}.$ After several minutes, the temperature of the liquid rose by $4.7^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C},$ at which point she decided to end the experiment. What was the final temperature of the liquid?" 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096464 1074 "To determine the final temperature of the liquid, we add the change in temperature to the initial temperature of the liquid. This gives\n\n$$\n-5.6^{\\circ}\\,\\textrm{C} + 4.7^{\\circ}\\,\\textrm{C} = -0.9^{\\circ}\\,\\textrm{C}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature of the liquid at the end of the experiment was $-0.9^{\\circ}\\,\\textrm{C}$." 13194139534973 17592186045417 false] [17592186096464 1074 "To determine the final temperature of the liquid, we add the change in temperature to the initial temperature of the liquid. This gives\n\n$$\n-5.6^{\\circ}\\,\\textrm{C} + 4.7^{\\circ}\\,\\textrm{C} = -0.9^{\\circ}\\,\\textrm{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature of the liquid at the end of the experiment was $-0.9^{\\circ}\\,\\textrm{C}.$" 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096465 1074 "To calculate the difference between the elevations of both cities, we must calculate the difference between the city with the highest elevation and the city with the lowest elevation. That is, we must calculate $83.2 - (-52.5)$. This gives\n\n$$\n83.2 - (-52.5) = 83.2 + 52.5 = 135.7 \\, \\textrm{ft}\n$$" 13194139534973 17592186045417 false] [17592186096465 1074 "To calculate the difference between the elevations of both cities, we must calculate the difference between the city with the highest elevation and the city with the lowest elevation. That is, we must calculate $83.2 - (-52.5).$ This gives\n\n$$\n83.2 - (-52.5) = 83.2 + 52.5 = 135.7 \\, \\textrm{ft}.\n$$" 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096466 1071 "In Chicago, the average temperature in December is $-1.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$. Then in January, the average temperature decreases by $3.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$ compared to December. Finally, in February, the average temperature increases by $2.6^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$ compared to the previous month. What is Chicago's average temperature in February?" 13194139534973 17592186045417 false] [17592186096466 1071 "In Chicago, the average temperature in December is $-1.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}.$ Then in January, the average temperature decreases by $3.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$ compared to December. Finally, in February, the average temperature increases by $2.6^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$ compared to the previous month. What is Chicago's average temperature in February?" 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096466 1074 "First, we must determine the temperature in Chicago during the month of January. Since we are told that the temperature *decreases* on average $3.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$ compared to December, we *subtract* the variation in temperature from the average temperature in December. This gives\n\n$$\n-1.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C} - 3.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C} = -5.0^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the temperature during the month of February. Since we are told that the temperature *increases* on average $2.6^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$ compared to January, we *add* the variation in temperature to the average temperature of January. This gives\n\n$$\n-5.0^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C} + 2.6^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C} = -2.4^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average temperature in Chicago in the month of February is $-2.4^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$." 13194139534973 17592186045417 false] [17592186096466 1074 "First, we must determine the temperature in Chicago during the month of January. Since we are told that the temperature *decreases* on average $3.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$ compared to December, we *subtract* the variation in temperature from the average temperature in December. This gives\n\n$$\n-1.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C} - 3.5^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C} = -5.0^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}.\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the temperature during the month of February. Since we are told that the temperature *increases* on average $2.6^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}$ compared to January, we *add* the variation in temperature to the average temperature of January. This gives\n\n$$\n-5.0^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C} + 2.6^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C} = -2.4^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average temperature in Chicago in the month of February is $-2.4^{\\circ} \\, \\textrm{C}.$" 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446305 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446310 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446303 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446309 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446302 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446307 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446308 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446304 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446306 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186096903 1050 540.0 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186097845 1064 "We can solve some real-world problems using addition and subtraction of real numbers.\n\n- We use addition when there is an increase in the data.\n\n- We use subtraction to find a difference, or when there is a decrease in the data.\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139534973 17592186045417 true] [17592186097845 1064 "We can solve some real-world problems using addition and subtraction of real numbers.\n\n- We use addition when there is an increase in the data.\n- We use subtraction to find a difference, or when there is a decrease in the data.\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139534973 17592186045417 false] [17592186446248 1080 "$-